19T09:21:00.696612", "methodology": "Registry", "name": "dppadpo-fatalityrateactivepko", "notes": "The Casualty Rate represents the proportion of fatalities among personnel in currently active United Nations peacekeeping operations (PKOs) from 2010 to present, calculated on a semi-annual basis (i.e., by half years).  \n  \nIt is calculated by comparing officially reported fatalities, received through the UN\u2019s Notification of Peacekeeper Casualties (NOTICAS) system, to the total number of uniformed personnel contributed by Member States. Rates are expressed per 1,000 personnel and computed only for PKOs that are currently active. The number of active PKOs is dynamically derived from the mission status (currently 11 active PKOs as of 1 December 2025, but this will automatically recalculate if the active missions change).  \n  \nThe dataset integrates two official UN sources:  \n  \nMission Fatalities \u2013 Fatalities in PKOs and SPMs since 1948.  \n  \nHistorical Uniformed Personnel Contribution", "num_resources": 1, "num_tags": 2, "organization": {"id": "8cb62b36-c3cc-4c7a-aae7-a63e2d480ffc", "name": "unpeacesecurity", "title": "United Nations Peace and Security Data Hub", "type": "organization", "description": "A free public library of datasets on peace and security published by the United Nations to empower data consumers across the world. The Peace and Security Data Hub is managed by the DPPA-DPO Information Management Unit to provide a centralized entry-point for up-to date public information on and about the UN\u2019s Peace and Security Pillar. The Hub was a direct outcome of the Secretary General\u2019s Data Strategy, which calls for ensuring everyone, everywhere can discover, access, integrate and share the data they need. The data on the platform is used by UN staff, UN Member States, journalists, training partners, academia and think tanks, as well as the public at large. With a growing number of datasets the Hub relies on an active user-base to provid